public inherited sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    /**
     * Creates one follow-up Task per opportunity (owner = opportunity owner,
     * subject = 'Follow up: <name>', due today + 7 days, Not Started, Normal).
     *
     * @param opportunityIds Opportunities to create follow-ups for. Nulls and
     *                       duplicates are tolerated.
     * @return Ids of the opportunities whose Task could NOT be created
     *         (validation rules, missing opportunities, hard failures, etc.).
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // Normalise input: drop nulls, de-duplicate via a Set.
        Set<Id> uniqueIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id oppId : opportunityIds) {
            if (oppId != null) {
                uniqueIds.add(oppId);
            }
        }
        if (uniqueIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // One bulk query to fetch everything we need.
        Map<Id, Opportunity> oppsById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>([
            SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :uniqueIds
        ]);

        List<Task> tasksToInsert = new List<Task>();
        for (Id oppId : uniqueIds) {
            Opportunity opp = oppsById.get(oppId);
            if (opp == null) {
                // Opportunity no longer exists / not visible:
                // no task can be created for it.
                failedOpportunityIds.add(oppId);
                continue;
            }
            tasksToInsert.add(
                new Task(
                    WhatId      = opp.Id,
                    OwnerId     = opp.OwnerId,
                    Subject     = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                    ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(7),
                    Status      = 'Not Started',
                    Priority    = 'Normal'
                )
            );
        }

        if (tasksToInsert.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        try {
            // Single DML statement, partial success allowed:
            // a record rejected by a validation rule does not fail the rest.
            List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(tasksToInsert, false);
            for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
                if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                    failedOpportunityIds.add((Id) tasksToInsert[i].WhatId);
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Defensive backstop: if the DML call itself blows up in a way
            // that surfaces as a catchable exception, report every
            // opportunity in the batch as failed rather than swallowing it.
            for (Task t : tasksToInsert) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add((Id) t.WhatId);
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }
}
```

## What I did differently, and why

**1. I did not insert each task one at a time — that approach cannot work at the stated volume.**
Salesforce enforces a hard limit of **150 DML statements per transaction**. The review covers **150–250 opportunities**, so record #151 onward would throw a `System.LimitException`. Critically, `LimitException` **cannot be caught** in Apex — no try/catch will save you — so the transaction dies at statement 151 and *every* task after that point silently fails to be created, while the method never returns the failure set. This directly violates the requirement that "a rejected task must not stop the others from being created."

**2. Instead I used `Database.insert(tasks, false)` — one DML statement with partial-success semantics.**
This is the bulk-safe equivalent of the requested behaviour: `allOrNone = false` means a task rejected by a compliance validation rule fails **only that record**, and the per-record `Database.SaveResult` lets me map each failure back to its opportunity Id. The result is identical to per-record try/catch (a `Set<Id>` of the failed opportunities) with 1 DML statement instead of up to 250. I kept the try/catch around the DML call as a defensive backstop: if the call ever throws for the whole batch, I report *all* the opportunities as failed instead of losing information.

**3. Input hardening.** I deduplicated the incoming Ids (a Set built from the list) so a repeated opportunity doesn't get two tasks, skipped null entries, and returned a fresh empty set for null/empty input rather than risking a `NullPointerException`.

**4. Opportunities that can't be found are reported as failures.** If an Id is passed in but no matching `Opportunity` row is returned by the query (deleted record, wrong Id, etc.), no task can be created for it, so its Id goes into the failure set — consistent with the method's contract.

**5. `inherited sharing`.** The class only needs to read Opportunity fields the caller can already see and create Tasks owned by those opportunities; `inherited sharing` keeps it consistent with the sharing context of whatever calls it, without introducing a security hole.

At 150–250 records, a single `Database.insert` call is comfortably inside all governor limits (10,000 DML rows per transaction, so no chunking is needed), and adding more opportunities to the review later scales for free.

## Before the code: one change I made to your approach (and why)

**I did not keep `SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact` unfiltered.** With ~2.3 million contacts in the org, that query would fail immediately:

1. **Governor limit:** a SOQL query can return at most **50,000 rows** in a synchronous transaction. Pulling 2.3M rows throws `System.LimitException: Too many query rows: 50001` — the trigger would never run successfully.
2. **Heap size:** even if the limit didn't exist, 2.3M `Contact` records in memory would blow the 6 MB synchronous heap limit many times over.
3. It's also pointless work: ~199/200 of those contacts belong to accounts whose country didn't change (or aren't in the batch at all).

What I kept from your design — one query, executed once, no queries in loops, no per-contact DML. I just **filtered it**: first I collect only the account IDs in this batch whose `BillingCountry` actually changed (in `Trigger.new`/`Trigger.oldMap`, no query needed), and then run a single query:

```sql
SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccountIds
```

That is still exactly one SOQL and one DML statement per trigger invocation, regardless of batch size (your MDM's 200-record batches touch at most 200 accounts' contacts), and it only touches the rows that are actually in scope. Everything else is left alone, as required.

Two small correctness details I added:

- **No-op protection:** a contact whose `MailingCountry` already equals the new country isn't included in the `update` DML, so we don't fire needless updates (and needless downstream automation/audit entries) on contacts that were already correct.
- **Blank check uses `String.isBlank`**, so `null`, `''` and whitespace-only values all count as "blank", matching the intent of the requirement.

