/**
 * Syncs contact MailingCountry when an account's BillingCountry changes.
 *
 * When an account's BillingCountry changes, contacts of that account whose
 * MailingCountry is blank or equal to the account's PREVIOUS BillingCountry
 * are updated to the new BillingCountry. Contacts with any other
 * MailingCountry (working abroad) and contacts of accounts whose country
 * did not change are left untouched.
 *
 * Design notes:
 * - Exactly ONE query per trigger invocation, filtered to the accounts in
 *   this transaction whose BillingCountry actually changed. An unfiltered
 *   "load all contacts" query is not viable: the org has ~2.3M contacts,
 *   far beyond the 50,000-row SOQL limit and the 6 MB heap limit.
 * - Exactly ONE DML statement per trigger invocation, on a bulkified list.
 * - No queries or DML inside loops; all lookups are done via maps/sets.
 */
public with sharing class AccountCountrySync {

    /**
     * @param newAccounts the Account records from Trigger.new (after update).
     * @param oldMap      the Account records from Trigger.oldMap (before update).
     */
    public static void sync(List<Account> newAccounts, Map<Id, Account> oldMap) {
        if (newAccounts == null || newAccounts.isEmpty() || oldMap == null || oldMap.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Step 1: identify only the accounts in this batch whose BillingCountry
        // actually changed. No query needed - old values are in oldMap.
        Map<Id, Account> changedAccounts = new Map<Id, Account>();
        for (Account newAccount : newAccounts) {
            Account oldAccount = oldMap.get(newAccount.Id);
            if (oldAccount == null) {
                continue;
            }
            if (newAccount.BillingCountry != oldAccount.BillingCountry) {
                changedAccounts.put(newAccount.Id, newAccount);
            }
        }
        if (changedAccounts.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Step 2: ONE query, executed once, filtered to contacts of the
        // changed accounts only. This is what keeps the trigger bulk-safe
        // while avoiding the 50k-row / heap limits of querying all contacts.
        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact contact : [
            SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccounts.keySet()
        ]) {
            Account changedAccount = changedAccounts.get(contact.AccountId);
            String previousCountry = oldMap.get(changedAccount.Id).BillingCountry;
            String newCountry = changedAccount.BillingCountry;

            // Only touch contacts whose MailingCountry is blank or matched
            // the account's previous country. Contacts with any other
            // MailingCountry are explicitly left alone.
            if ((String.isBlank(contact.MailingCountry)
                    || contact.MailingCountry == previousCountry)
                    && contact.MailingCountry != newCountry) {
                contact.MailingCountry = newCountry;
                toUpdate.add(contact);
            }
        }

        // Step 3: ONE bulkified DML statement.
        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update toUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

**How it behaves, briefly:**

- BillingCountry unchanged → account not even in the query filter; its contacts are never read or updated.
- Contact `MailingCountry` blank or equal to the *old* BillingCountry → updated to the new BillingCountry.
- Contact `MailingCountry` anything else (e.g., works abroad) → skipped, untouched.
- Batch of 200 accounts where only 5 changed country → the query only returns contacts of those 5 accounts; still one query, one `update`, well inside every governor limit.

If your org later adds logic to also run on `after insert` (new account created with a country), the handler already works unchanged — just add `after insert` to the trigger event list and guard the `oldMap` lookup (it would be `null` on insert).

